<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahendravarman (Chenla)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600</span> · Also known as Chitrasena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahendravarman, born Chitrasena, was a king of Chenla in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> around the end of the 6th and start of the 7th century. Together with his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavavarman-i">Bhavavarman I</a> he conquered much of Funan, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions across north-east Cambodia, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. He was succeeded by his son Ishanavarman I, who completed the takeover of Funan and built the capital Ishanapura.</p></div>
